{"name":"Caboose","hex":"#a8a4a1","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"caboose","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A small galley or cookhouse on the deck of a small vessel.","A small sand-filled container used as an oven on board ship."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/caboose"}],"entries":[{"title":"Caboose","blocks":[{"text":"A caboose is a crewed North American railroad car coupled at the end of a freight train. Cabooses provide shelter for crew at the end of a train, who were formerly required for rear-end protection in non-signaled territory, switching and shunting; as well as in keeping a lookout for load shifting, damage to equipment and cargo, and overheating axles."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ef/BN_caboose%2C_Eola_Yard%2C_1993.jpg/330px-BN_caboose%2C_Eola_Yard%2C_1993.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Caboose"},{"title":"Caboose (ship's galley)","blocks":[{"text":"A caboose is a small ship's kitchen, or galley, located on an open deck."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3c/Caboose_%28ship_stove%29.png/330px-Caboose_%28ship_stove%29.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Caboose_(ship's_galley)"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3a/Caboose_in_San_Diego.jpg/500px-Caboose_in_San_Diego.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3a/Caboose_in_San_Diego.jpg/1280px-Caboose_in_San_Diego.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3a/Caboose_in_San_Diego.jpg/500px-Caboose_in_San_Diego.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"An ex–Santa Fe Rail